<div class="moj_tekst">
  <h2>Creating a Java Project Without the OSP Library</h2>

  <p>
    In the following section, we will guide you through setting up a brand-new Java project in IntelliJ IDEA
    without relying on the OSP libraries. Instead, we’ll use <code>acm.jar</code> from the Stanford Java Task Force,
    which is ideal for simple graphical and educational applications.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Creating a New Java Project</h3>
  <p>
    First, we’ll set up the basic project structure inside IntelliJ IDEA.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      On the Welcome screen, click <strong>New Project</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the <em>New Project</em> dialog choose:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Language:</strong> Java</li>
        <li><strong>Build System:</strong> IntelliJ (Maven/Gradle not required)</li>
        <li><strong>Project SDK:</strong> select the JDK 11 you configured earlier</li>
      </ul>
      Then click <strong>Next</strong> and <strong>Finish</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      IntelliJ will open an empty project with a default <code>src</code> directory.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <hr />

  <h3>2. Adding the <code>acm.jar</code> Library</h3>
  <p>
    The ACM library makes it easy to draw and use interactive GUI components for educational purposes. Follow these steps to download and include it:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Download <code>acm.jar</code> from the Stanford site:
      <ul>
        <li>Visit the <a href="http://cs.stanford.edu/people/eroberts/jtf/" target="_blank">Stanford Java Task Force</a></li>
        <li>Under <strong>Java Libraries</strong>, click the <code>acm.jar</code> link directly:
          <a href="http://cs.stanford.edu/people/eroberts/jtf/acm.jar" target="_blank">acm.jar</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      In IntelliJ IDEA:
      <ul>
        <li>Right-click your project module → <em>Open Module Settings</em>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Dependencies</strong>, click <code>+ → JARs or directories…</code>.</li>
        <li>Select the downloaded <code>acm.jar</code> and confirm with <strong>Compile</strong> scope.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <hr />

  <h3>3. Brief Introduction to <code>acm.jar</code></h3>
  <p>
    <code>acm.jar</code> is a library developed by the Stanford team as part of the book  
    <em>The Art and Science of Java</em> (Eric Roberts). It’s designed for students to build simple graphical applications.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GraphicsProgram:</strong> base class for drawing on a canvas.</li>
    <li><strong>ConsoleProgram:</strong> interactive text input/output.</li>
    <li><strong>DialogProgram:</strong> basic dialog window.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Main advantages:
    <ul>
      <li>Simplified GUI components (window, canvas, buttons).</li>
      <li>Easy drawing of lines, circles, and text.</li>
      <li>Rapid development of educational simulations.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <h3>4. Example Java Application Using <code>acm.jar</code></h3>
  <p>
    The following code shows a simple <code>GraphicsProgram</code> that draws a circle on the screen:
  </p>
  <pre><code>import acm.program.*;
import acm.graphics.*;

public class PendulumWithoutOSP extends GraphicsProgram {
    public void run() {
        GOval circle = new GOval(100, 100, 50, 50);
        add(circle);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new PendulumWithoutOSP().start(args);
    }
}
  </code></pre>

  <p>
    After running successfully, a window with a circle will appear. From there, you can implement the pendulum animation
    using numerical integration and periodic screen updates.
  </p>

